/* ═══ SIDEBAR HUD — the character creator's register ══════════════════════════
   See plans/hud-creator-register.md (the binding contract). plans/phosphor-hud.md
   is the record of the cell-grid amber terminal this replaced.

   THIS SHEET IS AN OVERRIDE LAYER, NOT A VOCABULARY.

   sidebar_hud.rml links theme.rcss, then newchar_common.rcss, then this file.
   Every device on screen — the dotted rule, the pip meter, the fact block, the
   sigil-strip pair, the chip, the tally row, the column head — comes out of
   newchar_common.rcss unchanged, and this file only adds what the HUD needs on
   top: region positioning, the ink ladder, the list-row grid, and the CRT glass.
   Linking the creator sheet costs nothing the HUD does not use — every rule in
   it is class-scoped, there is not one bare-element selector in the file — and
   it is what makes the two surfaces impossible to drift apart. If a creator
   device is nearly right, override the one property here; do not copy the rule
   in and edit it.

   Four invariants govern this file:

   1. HIERARCHY IS LUMINANCE; GOLD IS A MARK, NEVER A DATUM. Producers may only
      colour with hud-i0..hud-i5, which are the shared gruvbox ladder
      (bg-hard, bg2, bg4, fg4, fg, fg0). Gold appears only from this stylesheet —
      the panel heads, the crit row's left edge, a filled pip, the alarm chip —
      and every one of those is redundant with a shape that survives greyscale.
      Add a gold rung to the ladder and you delete that property.

      (Do NOT write a token in its doubled-brace form inside a comment here:
      ui_theme::substitute_tokens rewrites the file as plain text and does not
      skip comments, so an example spelling is looked up like a real token, warns
      `unknown rcss token`, and lights RmlUi's beacon in the corner of the screen.
      That is a real 20x20px yellow badge this file put on screen for a while.)

   2. THERE IS NO `transition` ANYWHERE HERE, AND THERE MUST NOT BE. An RCSS
      transition wins on the property it names, and hud_anim writes `opacity` and
      `top` inline through Penner curves — the shipping HUD's
      `transition: opacity 0.8s` silently replaced every one of them. The
      `transition` .nc-pip brings from newchar_common.rcss is on
      background-color/height, touches no channel hud_anim writes, and is wanted.

   3. EVERY BOX STATES ITS OWN `display`. RmlUi elements default to
      `display: inline` and this game does not link RmlUi's sample rml.rcss. The
      shipping target-HP bar was a permanently empty trough for exactly this
      reason: a span with a percentage height had no `display`, and the inline
      default collapsed the box that percentage was of.

   4. THIS DOCUMENT CARRIES NEITHER `panel`, `runic-region` NOR `crt-overlay`.
      lighting::apply_crt() sweeps every open document for all three and drives
      their `decorator` inline every frame with the menus' warm runic ring
      (rmlui_layer.cpp:874-1070), which would overwrite this file's ground 60
      times a second.

   Backend facts, verified against the RmlUi 6.2 source this game vendors:
     · `text-shadow` does not exist; `font-effect: glow()` is the replacement and
       is applied in the font engine, so it works where a filter does not.
     · `backdrop-filter` parses but is INERT: lighting::rmlui_render_interface
       does not override PushLayer/PopLayer/CompositeLayers/CompileFilter. The
       alpha translucency below is real; the blur and desaturation are not. It is
       kept because it becomes live for free when the layer API lands.
     · `mask-image` is not inert but DESTRUCTIVE here — its gradient quads land
       on the swapchain over the panel's own text. The panel-edge dissolve is a
       linear-gradient on the ground for that reason, and because fading the
       ground leaves every glyph at full luminance.
     · Decorators paint over background-color and under content, and
       ElementEffects.cpp:195-200 walks the decorator list in REVERSE — so the
       scanline pass is listed FIRST to sit on top of the veil.
     · There is no border-style, so a dotted rule is a repeating gradient. That
       is .nc-rule's whole reason for existing; reuse it, never re-derive it.  */

body {
    background-color: transparent;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* The typeface is the one thing the HUD keeps from the terminal register:
       a monospaced face is what lets a column of values line up without a table.
       The SIZE is the creator's, not a derived cell metric — see §TYPE. */
    font-family: "Source Code Pro";
    font-size: 16dp;
    font-weight: normal;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    color: {{fg}};
}

/* ═══ REGIONS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Seven absolutely-positioned boxes. C++ writes left/top/width/height onto each
   from hud_runic::layout_for every frame the layout changes; nothing here may
   set those four, or the two would fight.                                     */

#hud-status,
#hud-soma,
#hud-radar,
#hud-dock,
#hud-log,
#hud-keys,
#hud-vehicle {
    position: absolute;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The two OPAQUE strips. They are opaque because they hold state a player must
   never misread — safe mode, hostiles, stats, needs; which action is on which
   key and which are unavailable — and a luminance-only encoding is only readable
   over a controlled ground. They are also the only two regions carved out of the
   terrain viewport, because they are the only two that hide what is under them. */
.hud-strip {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    padding: 4dp 8dp;
    decorator: repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
        {{hud-veil-0}} 0dp, {{hud-veil-0}} 2dp, {{hud-scan}} 2dp, {{hud-scan}} 3dp );
}

/* The floating panels. A 1dp border is the frame now: the cell grid that once
   made box-drawing glyphs the only way to close a corner is gone, so the frame
   is the thing CSS is actually good at.

   The veil gradient dissolves the panel's ground towards its BOTTOM edge, and
   the fade is vertical only: soma/dock/vehicle mirror with SIDEBAR_POSITION, so
   a fade hardcoded to a left or right edge points into the bezel the moment the
   player flips the sidebar. */
.hud-panel {
    border: 1dp {{bg4}};
    padding: 0;
    backdrop-filter: blur( 2.5dp ) saturate( 0.72 ) brightness( 0.94 );
    decorator:
        repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
            {{hud-veil-0}} 0dp, {{hud-veil-0}} 2dp, {{hud-scan}} 2dp, {{hud-scan}} 3dp ),
        linear-gradient( 180deg,
            {{hud-veil}} 0%, {{hud-veil}} 78%, {{hud-veil-0}} 100% );
}

/* The log dissolves UPWARD instead: the newest line — the one the player is
   actually reading — is on its last row, so fading the bottom would fade the
   only row that matters. */
#hud-log {
    decorator:
        repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
            {{hud-veil-0}} 0dp, {{hud-veil-0}} 2dp, {{hud-scan}} 2dp, {{hud-scan}} 3dp ),
        linear-gradient( 0deg,
            {{hud-veil}} 0%, {{hud-veil}} 78%, {{hud-veil-0}} 100% );
}

/* RADAR only. Its content is the GPU dot field drawn on the compositing layer
   UNDER this document; a veil over it would multiply the two dimmest dot
   categories into the ground and delete every open-terrain dot. hud_radar::draw
   paints its own ground fill instead. */
.hud-panel-clear {
    background-color: transparent;
    decorator: none;
}

/* …which leaves that panel's head with no ground of its own, sitting directly on
   live terrain. Give it one. */
.hud-panel-clear .nc-colhead {
    background-color: {{hud-veil}};
}

/* ═══ PANEL HEAD ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   .nc-colhead / .nc-col-sigil / .nc-colname come from newchar_common.rcss whole;
   only the padding and the type size are the HUD's.                           */

.nc-colhead {
    padding: 3dp 6dp 3dp 6dp;
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

.nc-colhead .nc-colname {
    font-size: 12dp;
    color: {{yellow}};
    /* The ONLY glow in this HUD, and only on the gold heads. It is theme.rcss's
       .title policy verbatim, including its reason: a glow under dense body text
       muddies it, and the glow token is bright gruvbox yellow — putting it on
       every row would tint the whole neutral ladder gold and undo the palette
       this register exists to adopt. */
    font-effect: glow( 2dp {{yellow-glow}} );
}

/* One procedural rune per panel, at the same 14dp the creator's column heads
   request. The seeds are arbitrary but FIXED: the generator is deterministic per
   seed, so changing one silently changes that panel's glyph. */
.nc-col-sigil.hud-sigil-soma {
    decorator: image( ?proc:runic-icon:14:7101:{{accent-rgb}} none contain ) border-box;
}

.nc-col-sigil.hud-sigil-radar {
    decorator: image( ?proc:runic-icon:14:7102:{{accent-rgb}} none contain ) border-box;
}

.nc-col-sigil.hud-sigil-dock {
    decorator: image( ?proc:runic-icon:14:7103:{{accent-rgb}} none contain ) border-box;
}

.nc-col-sigil.hud-sigil-log {
    decorator: image( ?proc:runic-icon:14:7104:{{accent-rgb}} none contain ) border-box;
}

.nc-col-sigil.hud-sigil-veh {
    decorator: image( ?proc:runic-icon:14:7106:{{accent-rgb}} none contain ) border-box;
}

/* ═══ PANEL BODY ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Scrolls. That is the whole overflow policy: a producer never counts rows
   against its region's height, because content taller than the region scrolls
   instead of painting across the region below it. The two exceptions are sized
   the other way round — the log's region is sized FROM its line count, and
   SOMA's effect roster is capped in the producer because pushing the pools
   behind a scrollbar the player cannot operate is worse than dropping the
   ninth effect.                                                               */

.hud-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: 2dp 6dp 4dp 6dp;
}

/* ═══ THE INK LADDER ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Six rungs, ground to peak, ≥38 relative-luma apart from ground through datum
   so the span survives greyscale and every form of colour blindness. peak sits
   only 20.7 above datum — that is the creator's own fg/fg0 relationship and it
   is deliberately NOT load-bearing: nothing critical is encoded by peak alone.

   THE LADDER SETS COLOUR AND NOTHING ELSE. A rung class always shares its
   element with a layout class (`<span class="hud-cell-name hud-i4">`), and both
   are single-class selectors, so a `display` here would be a coin toss decided
   by sheet order — and it would beat `.nc-fact-value`'s and `.nc-legend-label`'s
   `display: block` outright, since newchar_common.rcss is linked FIRST. Every
   box states its own display in its own layout rule; see invariant 3.         */

.hud-i0 { color: {{bg-hard}}; }
.hud-i1 { color: {{bg2}}; }
.hud-i2 { color: {{bg4}}; }
.hud-i3 { color: {{fg4}}; }
.hud-i4 { color: {{fg}}; }
.hud-i5 { color: {{fg0}}; }

/* The minimum a span needs to be a real flex item. RmlUi does NOT blockify flex
   children — the creator declares `display: block` on every one of its own span
   devices for exactly this reason — and an inline flex item silently ignores
   every width, flex and alignment property put on it. This is the default
   `value_class` on hud_runic::legend_item and the class on a subhead's text. */
.hud-text {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* ═══ LIST ROWS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Fixed 20dp, like the creator's own row lists, and for the same reason: the
   only way a column of name | meter | value reads as three columns is if every
   row is the same height. The transparent 3dp left border is reserved on EVERY
   row so that colouring it on a crit or a fresh log line shifts nothing.      */

.hud-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 20dp;
    line-height: 20dp;
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: transparent;
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: transparent;
}

/* The register's only shout, and it is the creator's cursor treatment rather
   than a second hue: gold left edge, dark fill, bold. Two of those three survive
   greyscale, so nothing here is encoded by colour alone. Red is deliberately not
   used — see plans/hud-creator-register.md §Palette. */
.hud-row.crit {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-left-color: {{yellow}};
    font-weight: bold;
}

.hud-subhead {
    font-size: 11dp;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

.hud-chiprow {
    height: 18dp;
    line-height: 18dp;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
}

/* .nc-chip carries a 4dp bottom margin for the creator's wrapping chip rows;
   inside a fixed-height row that margin overflows the row. */
.hud-chiprow .nc-chip {
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

.hud-cell-name {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 74dp;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The meter cell. .nc-pip is the creator's, including its filled-pips-are-taller
   rule, which is the second channel that keeps a meter readable in greyscale;
   only the sizes are the HUD's, because a 12dp pip does not fit a 20dp row. */
.hud-cell-meter {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-end;
    height: 9dp;
    margin: 0 8dp;
}

.hud-cell-meter .nc-pip {
    height: 4dp;
    margin-right: 1dp;
}

.hud-cell-meter .nc-pip.on {
    height: 9dp;
}

.hud-cell-val {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 74dp;
    text-align: right;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* .nc-tally scrolls on its own in the creator. Inside .hud-body, which already
   scrolls, a nested scroller traps the roster behind a scrollbar the HUD gives
   the player no way to drive. */
.hud-body .nc-tally {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    overflow-y: visible;
}

/* ═══ THE LIMB CARD AND ITS BODY FIGURE ═══════════════════════════════════════
   `hud_soma`'s limb section, collapsible. Only the LIMBS collapse — POOLS and
   EFFECTS below the card render in both states, because folding them away would
   be a content regression rather than a feature.

   THE FIGURE SITS BESIDE THE DETAIL ROWS, NOT ABOVE THEM, and that is a height
   decision. Side by side the pair costs max( rows 228, figure 78 ); stacked it
   would cost their sum and push POOLS and EFFECTS past the bottom of the region.
   That is not a deferred reading — it is a lost one. `.hud-body`'s scrollbar is
   decorative here: with only passive documents open, rmlui_layer.cpp:702-705
   feeds the wheel to RmlUi and then returns any_interactive_open() — false — so
   the event falls through to the map zoom and no HUD panel can be scrolled at
   all. Same reasoning as the roster cap and the disabled nested scroller above.

   Sizes here MUST agree with hud_soma_figure.h's fig_w / fig_h, which the height
   budget and the narrow-column fit are computed from.                        */

.hud-card {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.hud-fig-split {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

/* 46dp = 8 + 2 + 22 + 2 + 8 + 4 of margins; 78dp tall = 12 + 2 + 32 + 2 + 26 + 4. */
.hud-fig {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    width: 46dp;
    margin-right: 8dp;
    padding-top: 2dp;
}

.hud-fig-rows {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.hud-fig-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* The avatar seen from BEHIND, so a limb's side on screen matches its name and
   L ARM lights on the left — the figure sits directly beside rows labelled
   L ARM / R ARM and an anatomical chart's mirroring would contradict them. */
.hud-fig-head {
    display: block;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 12dp;
    margin-bottom: 2dp;
}

.hud-fig-torso {
    display: block;
    width: 22dp;
    height: 32dp;
    margin: 0 2dp;
}

.hud-fig-arm {
    display: block;
    width: 8dp;
    height: 28dp;
}

.hud-fig-leg {
    display: block;
    width: 10dp;
    height: 26dp;
    margin: 2dp 1dp 0 1dp;
}

/* Four rungs of the same ink ladder every producer uses, so the figure encodes
   health as LUMINANCE and survives greyscale. Intact recedes, damage advances.

   THE FLOOR IS bg4, NOT bg2, AND THAT IS MEASURED. An earlier draft started at
   bg2 (luma 74) to make a healthy limb recede; over this panel's own veil it
   receded out of existence — a near-full-health body, which is the common case,
   drew six boxes peaking at luma 69 and read as an empty panel. `ground` and the
   rung above it are both absent for the same reason: a limb filled with
   something indistinguishable from the background is a limb that has vanished,
   and this element exists precisely not to do that. */
.hud-fig-f1 { background-color: {{bg4}}; }
.hud-fig-f2 { background-color: {{fg4}}; }
.hud-fig-f3 { background-color: {{fg}}; }
.hud-fig-f4 { background-color: {{fg0}}; }

/* The same gold cursor mark .hud-row.crit and .hud-log-fresh carry — one device,
   three places, every one of them redundant with a shape. */
.hud-fig-crit {
    border: 1dp {{yellow}};
}

.hud-fig-worst {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* ═══ STATUS STRIP ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Two rows of three groups. The middle group is the elastic one: it wraps and
   clips, which is how a narrow viewport drops trailing fields. There is no width
   arithmetic in the producers and there must not be again — the cell-budget
   version of this strip is what truncated a player's name mid-word.           */

.hud-meta {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    height: 20dp;
}

.hud-meta-group {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.hud-meta-mid {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The .nc-fact-label recipe WITHOUT its colour, so the rung class the producer
   puts on the same element still wins. A caption in the status strip is chrome;
   shrinking and tracking it is what keeps the value beside it readable as the
   datum. */
.hud-lab {
    font-size: 11dp;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

/* The one gold-filled mark in the HUD: safe mode OFF, and overburdened. A filled
   chip, not a red word — the fill is the shape that survives greyscale. */
.hud-alarm {
    display: block;
    background-color: {{yellow}};
    color: {{bg-hard}};
    font-weight: bold;
    padding: 0 4dp;
}

/* ═══ MESSAGE LOG ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Recency is the luminance ramp across the rows; severity is the glyph. The two
   never contend, which is why neither needs the other's channel.

   `hud-log-entry` and the log-<seq> id on each row are load-bearing: hud_anim
   feeds and forgets rows by that exact id.                                    */

.hud-log-entry {
    position: relative;
}

/* The creator's cursor, doing the job the old `>` marker did. */
.hud-log-fresh {
    border-left-color: {{yellow}};
}

.hud-log-time {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 54dp;
}

.hud-log-glyph {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    text-align: center;
}

.hud-log-text {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* ═══ KEYS STRIP ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The creator's sigil strip, with a key glyph in the sigil position. `::` is its
   own device and replaces the old [n] index brackets, which carried nothing the
   key glyph did not.                                                          */

.hud-key {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-width: 22dp;
    font-weight: bold;
}

.hud-keyreason {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 6dp;
    font-size: 11dp;
}

/* .nc-legend brings 6dp/8dp/2dp padding and a 1dp top border from the creator.
   The border stays — it is the strip's edge against the map — and the padding
   goes, because .hud-strip already pads. */
#hud-keys .nc-legend {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    padding: 0;
    align-items: center;
}

#hud-keys .nc-legend-item {
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

/* Hidden until the avatar takes vehicle controls; the chassis toggles it. MUST
   come after the region block above, which sets `display: flex` on this id. */
#hud-vehicle {
    display: none;
}

/* ═══ CRT GLASS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Full-screen, no content, no hit-testing. The scanline pass makes the HUD and
   the world share one emissive surface instead of looking like text pasted onto
   a game; the faceplate is the honest half of a curved tube, since barrel
   distortion needs a shader and corner falloff does not.                      */

#hud-crt-scan,
#hud-crt-curve {
    position: absolute;
    display: block;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
}

#hud-crt-scan {
    z-index: 10;
    decorator: repeating-linear-gradient( 180deg,
        {{hud-veil-0}} 0dp, {{hud-veil-0}} 2dp,
        {{hud-scan-glass}} 2dp, {{hud-scan-glass}} 3dp );
}

/* Above the scanlines, so the falloff dims them too — which is what a real
   faceplate does. */
#hud-crt-curve {
    z-index: 11;
    decorator: radial-gradient( farthest-corner, {{hud-veil-0}} 42%, {{hud-vig}} 100% );
}

/* Damage flash. hud_anim drives `opacity` inline on total-HP loss, which is why
   there is no transition on it — see invariant 2. Last of all, so a hit is
   legible over the CRT glass. */
#hud-vignette {
    position: absolute;
    display: block;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 20;
    opacity: 0;
    decorator: radial-gradient( farthest-corner, {{hud-veil-0}}, {{yellow}} );
}
